index a2e5f76..46ccda5 100644
--- a/MOTION_RESEARCH.md
+++ b/MOTION_RESEARCH.md
@@ -59,3 +59,6 @@ The previous portal-based repair failed the owner's phone check. Remove it rathe
 The owner confirmed L42 visibility and supplied phone/desktop failure recordings. Phone selected-state/lens mismatch is confirmed; the exact runtime scheduler failure is not isolated. The shared custom RAF spring wrapper is replaced by the already installed Motion engine, preserving its consumer API, release velocity and current position. Navigation also resets on blur/background if a pointer-up is lost. Chromium selection/retarget/vertical and horizontal drag were exercised; device acceptance remains open.
 
 Remove global pointer-following radial lighting. Vertical skew is zero rather than an angle amplified by a wide row; desktop specimen width is constrained to sidebar proportions. The new optional menu has one clipped material viewport with bounds driven by one spring and unscaled foreground. This accepts layout work for a small menu instead of transforming a filtered sibling with WebKit occlusion artifacts. It is not compositor-only and no performance improvement is claimed. Chromium menu selection, Escape and interrupted closing/reopening succeeded. Actual iPhone fallback rendering and new gesture behavior require a new device recording.
+
+## L44 — repeated taps are not drag acquisition
+Pointer-down used jump(current) before detecting drag, stopping a travelling lens on every repeated tap. Tap release then supplied zero velocity. Navigation and segment now keep positional motion alive until the movement threshold is crossed, rebase from current presentation at acquisition, and retain same-target animations. This intentionally distinguishes a stationary held press from active dragging; press deformation still responds at pointer-down. Physical-device repeat/hold/reversal verification remains open.
diff --git a/VERIFICATION_L44.md b/VERIFICATION_L44.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..f3abd7e
--- /dev/null
+++ b/VERIFICATION_L44.md
@@ -0,0 +1,9 @@
+# L44 — repeated selection does not stop the lens
+
+Problem: pointer-down unconditionally jumped the positional spring to its current value, stopping travel even for a repeated tap. Pointer-up then restarted release with zero velocity. Repeated taps could continuously arrest movement.
+
+Change: retain positional animation during tap/hold; acquire position only when movement exceeds the existing 3px drag threshold. At that handoff, rebase the grab origin to the current presentation. A tap retargets with set(), preserving the existing spring for an unchanged target. A release to an already-running identical target does not restart the Motion animation. Applies to navigation and journal/demo segments. Cancellation and reduced-motion paths remain available.
+
+Validation: TypeScript and the existing ten numeric regression tests. These tests do not simulate repeated pointer input. Browser checks and physical-device scope are recorded in the release report. No new iPhone acceptance is claimed.
+
+Chromium preview: six repeated clicks on Demo committed once and the lens reached Demo; eight repeated clicks on the same vertical/horizontal navigation target committed once and settled at that target. Repeated click followed by a one-second held click did not leave the positional lens frozen at the starting tab. Drag from the last horizontal tab to the first still selected the first tab after threshold acquisition was corrected to discard only the 3px dead zone, not the entire first movement sample. These checks are desktop pointer checks, not physical iPhone acceptance.
